# JavaScript API Reference
{
    @meta {
        sdoc-version: 0.2
    }

    The SDOC parser and renderer are in `src/sdoc.js`. All functions are exported via CommonJS. The package is available on npm as `@entropicwarrior/sdoc`.

    # Installation
    {
        ```bash
npm install @entropicwarrior/sdoc
        ```

        Subpath exports are available for individual renderers:

        ```javascript
const { parseSdoc, extractMeta } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const { renderNotionBlocks } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc/notion");
const { renderSlides } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc/slides");
const { exportSlidePdf, exportDocPdf } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc/slide-pdf");
const { exportSlidePptx, buildPptx } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc/slide-pptx");
const { harvestGeometry, overflowReport } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc/slide-geometry");
const { loadTheme } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc/theme");
const { knowledgeDir } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc/knowledge");
        ```
    }

    # parseSdoc(text)
    {
        Parses an SDOC string into an AST.

        ```javascript
const { parseSdoc } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const { nodes, errors } = parseSdoc(sdocText);
        ```

        {[.]
            - `text` -- the SDOC source string
            - Returns `{ nodes, errors }` where `nodes` is an array of AST nodes and `errors` is an array of `{ message, line }` objects
        }
    }

    # extractMeta(nodes)
    {
        Extracts the `@meta` scope from parsed nodes and returns the remaining body nodes.

        ```javascript
const { parseSdoc, extractMeta } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const parsed = parseSdoc(text);
const { nodes, meta } = extractMeta(parsed.nodes);
        ```

        {[.]
            - `nodes` -- the AST node array from `parseSdoc`
            - Returns `{ nodes, meta }` where `meta` contains `stylePath`, `styleAppendPath`, `headerNodes`, `footerNodes`, `headerText`, `footerText`, and `properties`
            - `headerText` and `footerText` are set by key:value meta syntax (e.g., `header: My Header`)
            - `properties` is an object of arbitrary key:value pairs from the meta scope (e.g., `author`, `date`, `version`)
            - The reserved and `:comment` scopes written beside the root scope are moved into it in the returned `nodes`, as `foldRootSiblings` does, so a renderer sees a single root either way
        }
    }

    # getDocumentScope(nodes) and foldRootSiblings(nodes)
    {
        Find the document's root scope, which may have reserved scopes (`@meta` and the signposts) and `:comment` scopes written beside it.

        ```javascript
const { parseSdoc, getDocumentScope, foldRootSiblings } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const nodes = parseSdoc(text).nodes;
const root = getDocumentScope(nodes);   // the root scope node, or null
const body = foldRootSiblings(nodes);   // [root] with the scopes beside it as children
        ```

        {[.]
            - `getDocumentScope` returns the only top-level scope left once the reserved and `:comment` scopes are set aside, or `null` when any other top-level content (a paragraph, a second scope) means there is no single root
            - `foldRootSiblings` returns a one-element array holding a copy of the root, with the scopes written before it at the front of its children and those after it at the end. Without a single root, or with nothing beside it, it returns `nodes` unchanged. The parsed nodes are not modified
        }
    }

    # renderHtmlDocument(text, title, options)
    {
        Parses and renders a complete HTML page from SDOC source.

        ```javascript
const { renderHtmlDocument } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const html = renderHtmlDocument(sdocText, "Page Title", {
    config: { header: "My Header", footer: "My Footer" },
    cssOverride: customCssString,
    cssAppend: additionalCssString
});
        ```

        {[.]
            - `text` -- SDOC source string
            - `title` -- HTML page title
            - `options.config` -- object with `header` and `footer` strings
            - `options.cssOverride` -- replaces the default stylesheet
            - `options.cssAppend` -- appended after the base stylesheet
            - `options.includeSignposts` -- `true` keeps every signpost. By default only `@reading-guide` is kept. Set to `true` for preview-style rendering
            - `options.includeAbout` -- `true` keeps `@about` as well as the default. Ignored when `includeSignposts` is given
            - `options.signposts` -- keeps or drops single signposts, as a map from id to `true` or `false`, on top of the two options above. See **Signposts in exports** below
            - Automatically extracts and applies `@meta` scope settings
        }
    }

    # renderHtmlDocumentFromParsed(parsed, title, options)
    {
        Renders a complete HTML page from pre-parsed data. Use this when you need to parse and extract meta separately.

        ```javascript
const { parseSdoc, extractMeta, renderHtmlDocumentFromParsed } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const parsed = parseSdoc(text);
const metaResult = extractMeta(parsed.nodes);
const html = renderHtmlDocumentFromParsed(
    { nodes: metaResult.nodes, errors: parsed.errors },
    "Page Title",
    { meta: metaResult.meta }
);
        ```

        Accepts the same `options.includeSignposts`, `options.includeAbout` and `options.signposts` as `renderHtmlDocument`.
    }

    # Signposts in exports @signposts-in-exports
    {
        Signposts are the reserved scopes that describe a document rather than its subject: `@about`, `@not-about`, `@related-resources`, `@reading-guide` and `@edit-guide` (see [Signposts](sdoc-authoring.sdoc#signposts)). Four of them serve someone deciding whether to read, or someone editing the file. When a document is exported to HTML or PDF and sent to a recipient, that reader has already been asked to read it and will not edit it, so those four add noise. `@reading-guide` is written for exactly that reader.

        For that reason, **all HTML and PDF rendering paths drop every signpost except `@reading-guide` by default**. Only signposts at the top level (the root's children, or scopes beside the root) are dropped: a scope with a reserved id deeper down is an ordinary scope and is exported with its content, and `build-doc` and the VS Code export commands warn about it. This applies uniformly to `renderHtmlDocument`, `renderHtmlDocumentFromParsed`, `renderHtmlBody`, the `tools/build-doc.js` CLI, and the VS Code `sdoc.exportHtml` / `sdoc.exportPdf` commands. The live preview in the VS Code extension and the document browser pass `includeSignposts: true`, so authors see every section while editing and readers browsing a knowledge base see what each document covers and links to.

        Three options change which sections are kept. They are resolved in this order:

        {[#]
            - Start from the default: `@reading-guide` only.
            - `includeSignposts: true` keeps every section. When `includeSignposts` is not given, `includeAbout: true` keeps `@about` as well. An explicit `includeSignposts: false` keeps the default and ignores `includeAbout`.
            - `signposts` keeps (`true`) or drops (`false`) single sections on top of the result. Ids are matched case-insensitively. Unknown ids and values other than `true` and `false` are ignored.
        }

        ```javascript
// Default — only @reading-guide is kept (export-shape output)
const html = renderHtmlDocument(sdocText, "Title");

// Keep every signpost (preview-style rendering)
const html = renderHtmlDocument(sdocText, "Title", { includeSignposts: true });

// The default plus @related-resources, without @reading-guide
const html = renderHtmlDocument(sdocText, "Title", {
    signposts: { "related-resources": true, "reading-guide": false }
});

// Everything except @edit-guide
const html = renderHtmlDocument(sdocText, "Title", { includeSignposts: true, signposts: { "edit-guide": false } });
        ```

        Empty or whitespace-only signposts are never rendered, whatever the options say.

        **Helpers.** These are exported for custom renderers and tooling:

        {[.]
            - `SIGNPOSTS` -- the table behind all of this: per id, its default label (`title`), whether exports keep it (`exportVisible`), its content model (`model`) and, for structured sections, its table header (`table`)
            - `SIGNPOST_IDS` -- the five ids, in conventional order
            - `EXPORT_HIDDEN_SIGNPOST_IDS` -- the ids dropped from exports by default
            - `RESERVED_SCOPE_IDS` -- a `Set` of `meta` plus the five ids
            - `getSignpost(scope)` -- the `SIGNPOSTS` entry for a scope node, matched case-insensitively, or `null`
            - `isSignpostEmpty(scope)` -- `true` when a signpost has no children, or only whitespace-only paragraphs
            - `stripSignposts(nodes, ids)` -- a new node array with the given signposts removed from the top level (the root's children and the scopes beside the root); a scope with one of those ids deeper down is ordinary and stays; `ids` defaults to `EXPORT_HIDDEN_SIGNPOST_IDS`; the input is not mutated
            - `hiddenSignposts(options)` -- the ids a render with these options drops, resolved as above. `stripSignposts(nodes, hiddenSignposts(options))` is what the HTML renderers do
            - `isAboutEmpty(scope)` and `stripAboutScopes(nodes)` -- older names. `isAboutEmpty` is `isSignpostEmpty`; `stripAboutScopes` removes the top-level `@about` only
        }
    }

    # renderFragment(nodes, depth)
    {
        Renders an array of AST nodes to an HTML fragment (no `<html>` wrapper).

        ```javascript
const { parseSdoc, renderFragment } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const { nodes } = parseSdoc(text);
const html = renderFragment(nodes, 2);
        ```

        {[.]
            - `nodes` -- array of AST nodes
            - `depth` -- starting heading depth (default: 2)
        }
    }

    # renderTextParagraphs(text)
    {
        Renders a plain text string as HTML paragraphs with inline formatting. Used internally for header/footer rendering from config values.
    }

    # formatSdoc(text, indentStr)
    {
        Reformats an SDOC string by adjusting indentation based on brace depth. Structure is not changed -- only whitespace is modified.

        ```javascript
const { formatSdoc } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const formatted = formatSdoc(sdocText, "    ");
        ```

        {[.]
            - `text` -- the SDOC source string
            - `indentStr` -- the string to use for each indentation level (default: four spaces)
            - Returns the reformatted string
            - Code block content is passed through raw
            - Inline blocks (`{ content }`) are kept on one line
            - K&R style lines (heading ending with opener) are handled correctly
        }
    }

    # resolveIncludes(nodes, resolverFn)
    {
        Walks the AST and resolves code blocks with `src` metadata by loading the referenced file contents. This is an async function.

        ```javascript
const { parseSdoc, resolveIncludes } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const parsed = parseSdoc(text);
await resolveIncludes(parsed.nodes, async (src) => {
    return fs.readFileSync(path.resolve(docDir, src), "utf8");
});
        ```

        {[.]
            - `nodes` -- the AST node array to walk (modified in place)
            - `resolverFn` -- async function `(src) => string` that resolves a `src:` path to file contents
            - Applies `lines` range filtering if present on the code node
            - If the resolver throws, the code block text is set to an error message
        }
    }

    # Progressive Disclosure @progressive-disclosure
    {
        These functions enable token-efficient navigation of SDOC files. Instead of loading an entire file, an agent can discover what sections exist and then extract only the one it needs.

        All of these take the `nodes` that `parseSdoc(text)` returns, not the source text.

        # listSections(nodes)
        {
            Returns a flat list of every scope with a heading, at any depth, in document order. `@meta` and the signposts are excluded, with everything inside them. A scope with a reserved id below the top level is an ordinary scope and is listed.

            ```javascript
const { parseSdoc, listSections } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const sections = listSections(parseSdoc(sdocText).nodes);
// [{ id: "tables", derivedId: "tables", title: "Tables", scopeType: null, preview: "Opened with..." }, ...]
            ```

            {[.]
                - `nodes` -- the parsed nodes
                - Returns an array of `{ id, derivedId, title, scopeType, preview }` objects. `id` is the explicit `@id` or `null`; `derivedId` is the title through `slugify`, for addressing the section with `extractSection` (an `@ref` in a document does not resolve to it); `preview` is the start of the first paragraph
                - Use this as the "table of contents" step before extracting a section
            }
        }

        # extractSection(nodes, id)
        {
            Extracts a single section and its children by ID. This is the key function for surgical section retrieval.

            ```javascript
const { parseSdoc, extractSection } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const section = extractSection(parseSdoc(sdocText).nodes, "table-formulas");
// { title: "Table Formulas", content: "..." }
            ```

            {[.]
                - `nodes` -- the parsed nodes
                - `id` -- the section ID: an explicit `@id` (matched exactly), else a derived slug (matched case-insensitively)
                - Returns `{ title, content }`, plus `data` when the section holds data blocks, or `null` if not found
                - `content` is the plain text of the section and all its children
                - `@meta` and the signposts are never matched, nor anything inside them. A scope with a reserved id below the top level is ordinary and is matched
            }
        }

        # extractAbout(nodes)
        {
            Extracts the `@about` scope text -- a short summary of the document.

            ```javascript
const { parseSdoc, extractAbout } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const about = extractAbout(parseSdoc(sdocText).nodes);
// "Implementation status for the SDOC project. Tracks what is..."
            ```

            {[.]
                - `nodes` -- the parsed nodes
                - Returns the paragraphs of the top-level `@about` joined with a space, or `null`. List content is ignored; use `extractSignpost(nodes, "about")` to keep it
                - Use this for file discovery: list files with their `@about` summaries
            }
        }

        # extractSignpost(nodes, id)
        {
            Extracts any signpost as plain text.

            ```javascript
const { parseSdoc, extractSignpost } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const notAbout = extractSignpost(parseSdoc(sdocText).nodes, "not-about");
// "Slide decks: see the slide authoring guide.\n\nParser internals: not documented yet."
            ```

            {[.]
                - `nodes` -- the parsed nodes
                - `id` -- one of `about`, `not-about`, `related-resources`, `reading-guide`, `edit-guide`
                - Returns the section's paragraphs, list items and table rows joined with a blank line, inline markup left as written so links survive. Returns `null` when the id is not a signpost, or the section is absent or empty
                - Looks only at the top level: the root scope's children, and reserved scopes written beside the root
            }
        }

        # extractSignposts(nodes)
        {
            Returns every present, non-empty signpost as `{ [id]: text }`, each value as `extractSignpost` returns it, or `{}` when there are none.
        }

        # extractRelatedResources(nodes)
        {
            Reads the `@related-resources` table as data.

            ```javascript
const resources = extractRelatedResources(parseSdoc(sdocText).nodes);
// [{ label: "Specification", href: "../../lexica/specification.sdoc", kind: "sdoc",
//    relation: "Normative source for everything this guide teaches", lineStart: 21 },
//  { label: "https://github.com/entropicwarrior/sdoc", href: "https://github.com/entropicwarrior/sdoc",
//    kind: "external", relation: "Upstream repository", lineStart: 22 }]
            ```

            {[.]
                - Returns one object per row, or `null` when the document has no `@related-resources`
                - `kind` is `"sdoc"` for another SDOC document, `"file"` for any other file of the project, and `"external"` for a URL with a scheme. `resourceKind(href)` computes it on its own
                - `label` is the link text, or the URL itself for a bare URL
                - `lineStart` is the row's own source line
                - Rows that do not fit the schema are skipped, including a link into this document or to an absolute path. `validateSignposts` reports them
            }
        }

        # extractReadingGuide(nodes)
        {
            Reads `@reading-guide` as data: the free part, and the table that maps sections to notes.

            ```javascript
const guide = extractReadingGuide(parseSdoc(sdocText).nodes);
// { text: "Read the Quick Reference once, then ...",
//   entries: [{ sections: ["quick-reference"], note: "Everyday authoring", lineStart: 30 }] }
            ```

            {[.]
                - `text` -- the paragraphs and lists before the table, as plain text
                - `entries` -- one per well-formed row; `sections` holds the ids without the `@`, and `lineStart` is the row's own source line
                - Returns `null` when the document has no `@reading-guide`
            }
        }

        # slugify(title)
        {
            Converts a scope title to a URL-safe slug, matching the IDs derived by `listSections`.

            ```javascript
const { slugify } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
slugify("Table Formulas"); // "table-formulas"
            ```
        }

        # Typical agent workflow
        {
            ```javascript
            const { nodes } = parseSdoc(fileText);

            // 1. Discovery: list files with summaries (~200 tokens for 50 files)
            const about = extractAbout(nodes);

            // 2. Orientation: list sections of one file (~50-100 tokens),
            //    and how the author suggests reading them
            const sections = listSections(nodes);
            const guide = extractReadingGuide(nodes);

            // 3. Detail: extract just the section needed (~200-1000 tokens)
            const section = extractSection(nodes, "table-formulas");
            ```
        }
    }

    # validateSignposts(nodes) @validate-signposts
    {
        Checks the signposts against their content models. The VS Code extension shows the findings in the Problems panel, and `tools/build-doc.js --check` prints them.

        ```javascript
const { parseSdoc, validateSignposts } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const findings = validateSignposts(parseSdoc(sdocText).nodes);
// [{ type: "signpost-table", id: "related-resources",
//    message: "@related-resources table row 2: \"Resource\" must be exactly one link, [label](target) or a bare URL",
//    lineStart: 18, lineEnd: 22 }]
        ```

        {[.]
            - `nodes` -- the parsed nodes, before `extractMeta` so a misplaced `@meta` is caught too
            - Returns an array of findings in the same shape as `validateRefs`, plus a severity: `{ type, id, severity, message, lineStart, lineEnd }`. `severity` is `"warning"` for `signpost-order` and `"error"` for every other type. It never throws
            - `type` is one of `reserved-scope-placement` (the scope is then ordinary, and the message says so), `reserved-scope-duplicate`, `signpost-content`, `signpost-table`, `signpost-ref` (a `Sections` entry naming a reserved scope; an entry that does not resolve is left to `validateRefs`, which reports it as `broken-ref`), `signpost-order`. The rules are in [the specification](../../lexica/specification.sdoc#signpost-validation)
            - A finding about one table row carries that row's line, and the message names the row. A finding about the whole table, such as a wrong header, carries the table's lines
        }
    }

    # fixSignpostOrder(text) @fix-signpost-order
    {
        Puts `@meta` and the signposts in their conventional order, ahead of the body, the fix for a `signpost-order` warning. The VS Code quick fix **Reorder signposts** and `tools/build-doc.js --fix` both call it.

        ```javascript
const { fixSignpostOrder } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc");
const fix = fixSignpostOrder(sdocText);
if (fix.changed) fs.writeFileSync(file, fix.text);
else if (fix.reason) console.error(fix.reason);
        ```

        {[.]
            - `text` -- the SDOC source string; it takes text, not nodes, because it moves source lines
            - Returns `{ text, changed, reason }`. `changed` is `false` when the order was already right (`reason` is `null`) or when no safe fix exists (`reason` says why); `text` is then the input, unchanged
            - Each scope moves with the `//` comment lines directly above it and is re-indented to its new place. Line endings and the trailing newline are kept
            - A fix is returned only when it reparses to the same tree with just those scopes moved, keeps every non-blank line, and leaves no order warning
            - `RESERVED_ORDER` is the order it applies: `meta`, then the five signpost ids
        }
    }

    # AST Node Types
    {
        The parser produces these node types:

        {[.]
            - `scope` -- a section with `title`, `id`, `children`, `hasHeading`, and optional `task`
            - `paragraph` -- a text block with `text`
            - `list` -- a list with `listType` (`"bullet"` or `"number"`) and `items`
            - `blockquote` -- a quote with `paragraphs` (array of strings)
            - `table` -- a table with `headers` (array of strings), `rows` (array of string arrays), `rowLines` (the 1-based source line of each entry in `rows`, same order), and optional `options` (`{ borderless, headerless }`), `columnAlign`, `columnFormat`, and `columnCopy` (per-column booleans marking a copy column)
            - `code` -- a code block with `lang`, `text`, and optional `src` (file path/URL) and `lines` (`{ start, end }`). Special `lang` values: `mermaid` (rendered as diagram via Mermaid), `svg` (rendered as inline SVG with script/foreignObject stripping), `math` (rendered as display equation via KaTeX)
            - `citations` -- a citation definition block with `entries` (array of `{ key, text, lineStart, lineEnd }`)
            - `hr` -- a horizontal rule
        }

        Inline image nodes (`type: "image"`) within paragraphs include `src`, `alt`, and optional `width` (e.g. `"50%"`) and `align` (`"center"`, `"left"`, or `"right"`). Inline citation reference nodes (`type: "citation_ref"`) include `keys` (array of citation key strings). Inline hex color nodes (`type: "color_swatch"`) include `value` (the matched hex string, e.g. `"#9aa0a8"`) and render as a self-contained swatch. Inline copyable nodes (`type: "copyable"`) include `value` (the verbatim inner text) and render as monospace text with a click-to-copy icon.
    }

    # exportSlidePdf(htmlPath, pdfPath)
    {
        Exports an HTML file to PDF using headless Chrome with 16:9 landscape dimensions (slide format).

        ```javascript
const { exportSlidePdf } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc/slide-pdf");
await exportSlidePdf("/tmp/slides.html", "/tmp/slides.pdf");
        ```

        {[.]
            - `htmlPath` -- path to the HTML file to convert
            - `pdfPath` -- path for the output PDF
            - Returns a Promise that resolves with the output path
            - Requires Chrome or Chromium (auto-detected, or set `CHROME_PATH`)
        }
    }

    # exportDocPdf(htmlPath, pdfPath)
    {
        Exports an HTML file to PDF using headless Chrome with A4 portrait dimensions (document format).

        ```javascript
const { exportDocPdf } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc/slide-pdf");
await exportDocPdf("/tmp/document.html", "/tmp/document.pdf");
        ```

        {[.]
            - `htmlPath` -- path to the HTML file to convert
            - `pdfPath` -- path for the output PDF
            - Returns a Promise that resolves with the output path
            - Uses A4 paper (8.27 x 11.69 inches) with print-optimized styles
        }
    }

    # loadTheme(themeDir, fallbackDir)
    {
        Reads a slide theme directory: `theme.css`, optionally `theme.js`, optionally `theme.json`.

        Relative `url()` references in the CSS are rewritten to `data:` URIs so a built deck is one
        self-contained file. A path escaping the theme directory is refused and reported.

        ```javascript
const { loadTheme } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc/theme");
const { themeCss, themeJs, themeConfig, warnings } = loadTheme("themes/mine", "themes/default");
const html = renderSlides(nodes, { meta, themeCss, themeJs, themeConfig });
        ```

        {[.]
            - `themeDir` -- the theme directory to read
            - `fallbackDir` -- supplies `theme.js` when the theme ships none (optional)
            - Returns `{ themeCss, themeJs, themeConfig, warnings, dir }`
            - `themeConfig` carries the design box (`slide`), print page (`page`), default `fit` mode and PPTX `fonts`
        }
    }

    # harvestGeometry(htmlPath, options)
    {
        Measures a built HTML deck in headless Chrome: every box, rule, image and run of text, with
        its position and computed style in design-box pixels. This is what drives the PowerPoint
        export, so the CSS stays the only source of layout truth.

        ```javascript
const { harvestGeometry, overflowReport } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc/slide-geometry");
const geometry = await harvestGeometry("/tmp/deck.html");
for (const finding of overflowReport(geometry)) {
    console.error(finding.slide, finding.over.join(", "));
}
        ```

        {[.]
            - `htmlPath` -- a built deck, in a directory where its relative assets resolve
            - `options.attempts` -- tries before giving up (default 3)
            - `options.timeoutMs` -- base deadline per attempt, which grows with each one (default 30000)
            - Returns a Promise for `{ box, slides }`
            - Requires Chrome or Chromium (auto-detected, or set `CHROME_PATH`)
        }

        `overflowReport(geometry, options)` returns one finding per slide whose content has left the
        margin the theme reserved. Pass `{ strict: false }` to report only content clipped by the
        design box.
    }

    # exportSlidePptx(htmlPath, pptxPath, options)
    {
        Measures a built HTML deck and writes a PowerPoint file beside it. Drive imports the result
        as a native Google Slides deck.

        ```javascript
const { exportSlidePptx } = require("@entropicwarrior/sdoc/slide-pptx");
const { path, slides, skippedImages } = await exportSlidePptx("/tmp/deck.html", "/tmp/deck.pptx", {
    title: "Q3 Review",
    fonts: { display: "Georgia", body: "Helvetica" }
});
        ```

        {[.]
            - `htmlPath` -- a built deck
            - `pptxPath` -- path for the output `.pptx`
            - `options.title` -- deck title, for the file's document properties
            - `options.fonts` -- `{ display, body }` typefaces used when a CSS stack resolves to a generic family
            - Returns a Promise for `{ path, slides, skippedImages, geometry }`
            - Requires Chrome or Chromium
        }

        `buildPptx(geometry, options)` is the same conversion without the browser step, for a geometry
        object already in hand. It returns `{ buffer, skippedImages }`.
    }
}
